    <h1>About</h1>
  </div>

  <div class="about-section">
    <h2>Official Bio</h2>
    <p>Morgan Travis is a customer operations and support leader with 13 years building customer-facing teams in complex, regulated environments. As a Senior Manager at AWS Enterprise Support, he oversaw operations serving 400+ enterprise customers across government, financial services, and healthcare, managing technical account managers, escalation workflows, and the systems that kept them running.</p>
    <p>His work focuses on applying DevOps and lean rigor to support operations: measurement frameworks, coaching infrastructure, and the operating models that let teams deliver consistently at scale. He has led AI readiness assessments, built automation pipelines for enterprise support workflows, and developed the Support Operations framework, a direct translation of DORA software delivery research for customer-facing teams.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="about-section">
    <h2>Personal Bio</h2>
    <p>I am a fixer. I get dropped into situations where a team cares deeply but the systems have not caught up, and I build the infrastructure to match the intention.</p>
    <p>I have done this in enterprise cloud support, in digital consulting, in government contracting, and in civil engineering finance. The problems look different. The pattern is the same: good people, unclear measurement, no operating model, and a belief that if you just hire more people the quality will improve.</p>
    <p>It does not, but you can build a lean system that does.</p>
    <p>I write about Support Operations: the application of DevOps rigor to customer-facing teams. There is no published coherent framework for this that is not vendor specific, so I am writing it.</p>
  </div>

